{:title "How a plane cuts a cone (I.11-13)"
 :source "history-of-math/notebooks/apollonius_conics.clj, Notation, fixed once"
 :steps
 [{:id "S1"
   :text "The cone is the surface traced by a line through a fixed point, the **apex**, as the line runs round a circle."
   :introduces [:apex :circle :generator :cone]}
  {:id "S2"
   :text "Continued past the apex, it makes a **double cone** of two **nappes**."
   :introduces [:generator2 :nappe2]}
  {:id "S3"
   :text "The **axial triangle** $ABC$ is the cut through the apex $A$ and a diameter $BC$ of the base circle."
   :introduces [:axial :AB :AC :BC :A :B :C]}
  {:id "S4"
   :text "The cutting plane meets the axial triangle in a line $PM$, the **diameter** of the section. $P$ is its **vertex**."
   :introduces [:plane :PM :P :M :section]}
  {:id "S5"
   :text "A chord of the section parallel to the base line $DE$ is bisected by $PM$. Half of it, $QV$, is an **ordinate**; we write $y = QV$."
   :introduces [:DE :D :E :chord :V :QV :Q :y]}
  {:id "S6"
   :text "The piece $PV = x$ of the diameter is the **abscissa**."
   :introduces [:PV :x]}
  {:id "S7"
   :text "$p$ is a length fixed by the cone and the plane, the **parameter** (Apollonius' \"upright side,\" Latin *latus rectum*)."
   :introduces [:PL :L :p]}
  {:id "S8"
   :text "$d = PP'$ is the **transverse diameter**, when the plane meets the opposite side of the axial triangle again at $P'$."
   :introduces [:AC' :PP' :P' :d]}]}
